**Proof.**

Place the center $M$ of the sphere at the origin, and let the plane of $ABC$ be $z=0$. The sphere is then
$$|X|^{2}=R^{2},$$
and since $A,B,C$ lie on the sphere and on $z=0$, we have
$$|A|^{2}=|B|^{2}=|C|^{2}=R^{2},\qquad A,B,C\in z=0.$$
Let $S$ be the fourth vertex ($S\notin z=0$), and set
$$P:=|S|^{2}-R^{2}\qquad(\text{the power of }S\text{ w.r.t. the sphere}).$$
The configuration is non‑degenerate, so $P\neq 0$.

---

**The second intersections.** Parametrize the line $SA$ as $X=S+t(A-S)$. Substituting into $|X|^{2}=R^{2}$ gives a quadratic in $t$ with one root $t=1$ (the point $A$); the product of the roots equals $(|S|^{2}-R^{2})/|A-S|^{2}=P/SA^{2}$. Hence the other root is
$$t_{A}=\frac{P}{SA^{2}},\qquad A_{1}=S+t_{A}(A-S),$$
and similarly $B_{1}=S+t_{B}(B-S)$, $C_{1}=S+t_{C}(C-S)$ with $t_{B}=P/SB^{2}$, $t_{C}=P/SC^{2}$.

---

**The tangent planes and the point $O$.** The tangent plane to $|X|^{2}=R^{2}$ at $A_{1}$ is
$$A_{1}\cdot X=R^{2},$$
and likewise at $B_{1},C_{1}$. The point $O$ is their common intersection, so
$$A_{1}\cdot O=B_{1}\cdot O=C_{1}\cdot O=R^{2}. \tag{1}$$

From (1), using $|A_{1}|^{2}=R^{2}$,
$$|O-A_{1}|^{2}=|O|^{2}-2A_{1}\cdot O+|A_{1}|^{2}=|O|^{2}-2R^{2}+R^{2}=|O|^{2}-R^{2},$$
and identically $|O-B_{1}|^{2}=|O-C_{1}|^{2}=|O|^{2}-R^{2}$. Thus $O$ is already equidistant from $A_{1},B_{1},C_{1}$. It remains to prove
$$|O-S|^{2}=|O|^{2}-R^{2},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad O\cdot S=\frac{|S|^{2}+R^{2}}{2}. \tag{2}$$

---

**Computing $O\cdot S$ via the plane $A_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$.** Since $S\notin z=0$, the vectors $A-S,\,B-S,\,C-S$ form a basis of $\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Define an affine function $L$ by writing the unique decomposition
$$X-S=u(A-S)+v(B-S)+w(C-S),\qquad L(X):=\frac{u}{t_{A}}+\frac{v}{t_{B}}+\frac{w}{t_{C}}.$$
Then $L(S)=0$ and $L(A_{1})=L(B_{1})=L(C_{1})=1$, so
$$\text{plane }A_{1}B_{1}C_{1}=\{X:L(X)=1\}.$$
Because $L(S)=0$, there is a vector $\ell$ with $L(X)=\ell\cdot(X-S)$, i.e.
$$\ell\cdot(A-S)=\frac{1}{t_{A}}=\frac{SA^{2}}{P}=\frac{R^{2}-2A\cdot S+|S|^{2}}{P},$$
and similarly for $B,C$.

Let $\mathbf{n}$ be the vector perpendicular to the plane $ABC$ normalized so that $\mathbf{n}\cdot S=1$ (equivalently $\mathbf{n}\cdot A=\mathbf{n}\cdot B=\mathbf{n}\cdot C=0$, $\mathbf{n}\cdot S=1$). We claim
$$\ell=-\frac{2}{P}\,S+\mathbf{n}.$$
Indeed,
$$\Bigl(-\frac{2}{P}S+\mathbf{n}\Bigr)\cdot(A-S)=-\frac{2}{P}(A\cdot S-|S|^{2})+(\mathbf{n}\cdot A-\mathbf{n}\cdot S)=\frac{2(|S|^{2}-A\cdot S)}{P}+(0-1)$$
$$=\frac{2|S|^{2}-2A\cdot S-P}{P}=\frac{|S|^{2}+R^{2}-2A\cdot S}{P}=\frac{SA^{2}}{P},$$
and the same holds for $B,C$. Hence the claim, and therefore
$$\ell\cdot S=-\frac{2}{P}|S|^{2}+\mathbf{n}\cdot S=-\frac{2|S|^{2}}{P}+1=\frac{P-2|S|^{2}}{P}=-\frac{|S|^{2}+R^{2}}{P}. \tag{3}$$

---

**The pole of $A_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$.** The plane $L(X)=1$ reads $\ell\cdot(X-S)=1$, i.e.
$$\ell\cdot X=1+\ell\cdot S=1-\frac{|S|^{2}+R^{2}}{P}=\frac{P-|S|^{2}-R^{2}}{P}=-\frac{2R^{2}}{P}.$$
By (1), $A_{1},B_{1},C_{1}$ all lie on the plane $O\cdot X=R^{2}$ (the polar of $O$); hence this plane coincides with $A_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$. Comparing $\ell\cdot X=-2R^{2}/P$ with $O\cdot X=R^{2}$ gives
$$O=-\frac{P}{2}\,\ell.$$
Using (3),
$$O\cdot S=-\frac{P}{2}\,(\ell\cdot S)=-\frac{P}{2}\Bigl(-\frac{|S|^{2}+R^{2}}{P}\Bigr)=\frac{|S|^{2}+R^{2}}{2},$$
which is exactly (2).

---

**Conclusion.** From (2),
$$|O-S|^{2}=|O|^{2}-2O\cdot S+|S|^{2}=|O|^{2}-(|S|^{2}+R^{2})+|S|^{2}=|O|^{2}-R^{2}=|O-A_{1}|^{2}.$$
Together with the equalities $|O-A_{1}|^{2}=|O-B_{1}|^{2}=|O-C_{1}|^{2}=|O|^{2}-R^{2}$, this yields
$$|O-S|=|O-A_{1}|=|O-B_{1}|=|O-C_{1}|.$$
Therefore $O$ is equidistant from all four vertices of the tetrahedron $SA_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$; i.e. $O$ is the circumcenter of $SA_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$. $\blacksquare$

